---
source: crates/standalone/fixture/src/lib.rs
---
Turn a folder of writing into a site, and publish it.

Usage: ink [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Examples
  ink check                 # Read this folder and list its problems
  ink build -o public       # Build into ./public
  ink explain posts/a.md    # Why one file looks the way it does
  ink select notes/trip.md  # Publish one page of a vault

Write
  check, c  Read the folder and report what is wrong; writes nothing
  build, b  Build the folder into an output folder and print its deploy id
  preview   Build in memory and serve it on 127.0.0.1 while you write

Understand
  explain  Show the decisions behind the site, or behind one file
  pin      Write one decision's value into ink.toml
  select   List what is published and why, or choose what to publish

Publish
  init    Link this folder to a site, without publishing it
  push    Send the folder to its site and start a deploy
  status  Show what is live and what is building
  watch   Follow a build of this site to its end
  domain  Claim, prove, list and remove custom domains
  repo    Claim, prove, rotate, list and release the site's repository
  deploy  List, read, roll back and resume deploys
  export  Download the site, or the files the last push sent, to a folder

Account
  login   Sign in to uses.ink
  logout  Sign out: end this machine's key
  whoami  Show who you are signed in as
  key     List, make and end keys
  org     Make organizations and manage who is in them

Operate
  serve  Serve a built deploy the way production does
  abuse  Read reports, suspend sites and lift suspensions

Tools
  completions  Print a completion script for your shell
  man          Print the manual page
  help         Show this message, or the help of one command

Global options
  -C, --dir <DIR>        The site's folder [default: .] [env: INK_DIR]
      --json             One JSON document on stdout, diagnostics as NDJSON on
                         stderr
  -q, --quiet...         Say less: `-q` drops stage lines and notices and keeps
                         warnings, the result and what a build makes public;
                         `-qq` leaves only errors
  -v, --verbose...       Say more: -v adds notices, -vv adds every file
      --color <WHEN>     When to colour output, decided for each stream: `auto`
                         follows `NO_COLOR`, `CLICOLOR`, `CLICOLOR_FORCE`,
                         `TERM` and whether the stream is a terminal
                         [default: auto] [env: INK_COLOR]
      --no-progress      Print log lines only, with no live redraw of progress
                         [env: INK_NO_PROGRESS]
      --log <FILTER>     Log to stderr as `ink=debug,edge=trace` says: areas
                         (ink, engine, build, edge, control, db, blob, host,
                         plugin, watch, queue, cli) or targets, at a level. Off
                         unless this, `-v` or `INK_LOG` asks [env: INK_LOG]
      --log-file <PATH>  Also write the log as JSON lines to this file
                         [env: INK_LOG_FILE]
  -h, --help             Print help; -h is short and --help is long
  -V, --version          Print the version

Environment
  INK_DIR          The site's folder, as -C
  INK_COLOR        auto, always or never, as --color
  INK_NO_PROGRESS  Log lines only, as --no-progress
  INK_LOG          The log filter, as --log; RUST_LOG is not read
  INK_LOG_FILE     Also write the log as JSON lines here, as --log-file
  INK_LOG_FORMAT   pretty, compact or json, on stderr
  INK_LOG_SPANS    none, open, close or full
  INK_LOG_TIMINGS  1 prints the span timing table at the end
  NO_COLOR         Set and not empty: no colour
  CLICOLOR_FORCE   Set and not 0: colour even to a pipe
  INK_COMPONENTS   The folder the build script reads components from

Read `ink help <command>` for one command and `ink help exit` for the exit
codes.
